# November 21, 2026: a first scatter of places across the rest of Nebraska, the state with the fewest places on the map
# (20 before this batch, nearly all in the Missouri River towns of the southeast). Claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest (ne).
# Sources read in this session: the Wikipedia articles (tools/wiki_raw.py); National Park Service "places" pages for the
# Freeman School, Rock Creek Station and Fort Kearny (page.py); History Nebraska's own pages for Neligh Mill, the Norris
# House, the Kennard House and the Fort Robinson History Center (read with the web fetch tool, because page.py gets 403
# there); a Nebraska Game and Parks news item on Fort Hartsuff; the Visit North Platte page on the Buffalo Bill Ranch; the
# Willa Cather Foundation's hours and tours pages; Joslyn Castle's tours page; the Durham Museum's hours page; the Trailside
# Museum's visit page; the Western Nebraska Tourism Coalition's Fort Sidney page; a June 2026 WOWT report on Neligh Mill.
# outdoornebraska.gov (Game and Parks) answers 403 to page.py, so most state park hours were not read: those say so.
# Red brick: the Freeman School is red brick by the Homestead article; Union Station (cream terra cotta), Joslyn Castle
# (limestone), the Norris House (stucco on wood), the Kennard House (stuccoed brick), the Cather house and Champion Mill
# (frame), the Dowse house (sod) and Fort Kearny (sod and adobe reconstructions, an earthwork) are not. Fort Robinson is 'some': red brick Comanche Hall and riding stable in Commons photographs looked at with photo_find.py. The rest are '?'.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'ne_fhrx','label':'Nebraska: a first scatter across the state','added':'2026-11-21 21:28'}

HN=lambda slug,label: ['History Nebraska: '+label,'https://history.nebraska.gov/'+slug]
NPSP=lambda slug,label: ['National Park Service: '+label,'https://www.nps.gov/places/'+slug+'.htm']
NGPC='Nebraska Game and Parks Commission'
PERMIT='A Nebraska park entry permit is needed for a vehicle in a state park. '

PLACES=[
N('fort-hartsuff-burwell','Fort Hartsuff','Burwell','ne','fort','standing',41.72278,-99.02306,'site','open','drive','forts','1874–1881',
  'A U.S. Army cavalry outpost of the late Indian Wars, active from 1874 to 1881 on the North Loup River and named for George Lucas Hartsuff, a Union major-general of volunteers. The state took it on as a historical park in 1961.',
  'Original buildings round the parade ground: the post headquarters, enlisted men\'s barracks, officers\' quarters, commanding officer\'s quarters and post hospital (Wikipedia); History Nebraska counts nine original buildings standing. Other buildings are reconstructions. Interior not checked beyond a Game and Parks notice that the historical buildings open on event days.',
  '',
  'A state historical park of the '+NGPC+', about 6 miles southeast of Burwell. A Game and Parks notice for a cannon-firing day gave building hours of 11 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. that day, a small building fee and the park entry permit; the ordinary season and hours were not read (outdoornebraska.gov refuses this session\'s tools). Parking was not checked: no lot is mapped.',
  [W('Fort Hartsuff State Historical Park'),HN('fort-hartsuff-state-historical-park/','Fort Hartsuff'),['Nebraska Game and Parks: cannon firings at Fort Hartsuff','https://outdoornebraska.gov/?p=127154']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('neligh-mill','Neligh Mill','Neligh','ne','industry','standing',42.1264,-98.03118,'area','open','drive','','1873–1969',
  'A water-powered flour mill on the Elkhorn River, begun in brick by the town\'s founder John Neligh in 1873 and finished by W. C. Gallaway, grinding from August 1874. It changed from millstones to steel rollers in 1886, made electricity for the town until 1925, and closed in 1969. History Nebraska runs it as a museum.',
  'The brick mill with its milling machinery: History Nebraska calls it the only 19th-century mill in the state that keeps all its original equipment. The restored mill office with its furnishings, the reconstructed flume and penstock, and exhibits in the old warehouse and the 1915 power-plant addition. The mill has no heat.',
  'The one Nebraska flour mill of the 1870s still holding all its machinery.',
  'State historic site of History Nebraska at N Street and Wylie Drive; admission free (its own page, read November 2026). Off season it asks visitors to call ahead for an appointment and dress warm. Street parking nearby; parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Neligh Mill'),HN('neligh-mill-state-historic-site/','Neligh Mill State Historic Site'),['WOWT: historic flour mill (June 2026)','https://www.wowt.com/2026/06/23/historic-nebraska-flour-mill-preserves-19th-century-milling-heritage/']],brick='?',
  hours='May 18 to Sept 5: Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; Sept 6 to May 17: Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., call ahead'),
N('fort-robinson-crawford','Fort Robinson','Crawford','ne','fort','standing',42.66837,-103.46635,'site','open','drive','forts','1874',
  'Founded in 1874 beside the Red Cloud Agency in the Pine Ridge country and named Fort Robinson in 1878, it was a base for the Sioux Wars; Crazy Horse died of his wounds in the adjutant\'s office in September 1877, and the Cheyenne breakout of 1879 ended in the Fort Robinson massacre. Some buildings were pulled down in the mid-1950s, when the state historical society began buying the fort and opened a museum in 1956. It is a National Historic Landmark inside a 22,000-acre state park.',
  'History Nebraska\'s Fort Robinson History Center is in the 1905 post headquarters; its page also lists the 1904 blacksmith shop, the 1908 veterinary hospital, the 1887 adobe officers\' quarters, the 1875 guardhouse and adjutant\'s office (rebuilt; a plaque marks where Crazy Horse died), the old post cemetery and the prisoner-of-war camp site. The University of Nebraska\'s Trailside Museum is in the 1904 post theatre. Which buildings open beyond the two museums was not checked.',
  '',
  'Fort Robinson State Park, run by the '+NGPC+' with buildings run by History Nebraska and the University of Nebraska; 2 miles west of Crawford on U.S. 20. '+PERMIT+'History Center admission charged (its own page, read November 2026); the Trailside Museum is open Wednesday to Sunday 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. until its season ends October 31 (its own page). Parking: the lot on U.S. 20 by the History Center.',
  [W('Fort Robinson'),HN('?p=368842','Fort Robinson History Center'),['Trailside Museum: visit','https://dxg-trailside.unl.edu/visit.html']],th=[42.66749,-103.46449],thn='Lot on U.S. 20 by the History Center',brick='some',
  hours='History Center: May 1 to Sept 2 daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Sept 3 to April 30 Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('dowse-sod-house-comstock','Dowse Sod House','Comstock','ne','landmark','standing',41.51108,-99.26131,'site','open','drive','','1900–1959',
  'A sod house built in 1900 by William R. Dowse in Custer County, the county Solomon Butcher photographed sod houses in, even though the railroad and a lumber yard had reached Comstock the year before. The family lived in it until 1959; it was restored from about 1981 and opened as a museum in 1982. The Register calls it an excellent example of the sod house, one of few left in Nebraska.',
  'Walls of sod blocks laid grass side down, 27 inches thick at the ground and battered to 20 at the top; the west wall was taken down in 1924. Interior not checked.',
  'A homesteader\'s house built of prairie sod and lived in until 1959.',
  'A museum near Comstock; who runs it and when it opens were not found on a page of its own in this session. The pin is the Wikipedia coordinate. Parking not checked: no lot is mapped.',
  [W('Dowse Sod House')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('freeman-school-beatrice','Freeman School','Beatrice','ne','civic','standing',40.29218,-96.84211,'exact','public','drive','','1872–1967',
  'School District No. 21, a one-room school of foot-thick red brick with carved limestone lintels, used from 1872 to 1967, the longest a one-room school ran in Nebraska. It also served as a Lutheran meeting place and the Blakely Township polling place. It may be named for Thomas Freeman, a bricklayer, or Daniel Freeman, the homesteader, who both sat on its board.',
  'The National Park Service has restored the school to look as it did in the 1870s. Whether it opens inside, and when, was not checked.',
  'A one-room brick schoolhouse used for 95 years on the edge of the first homestead claims.',
  'Part of Homestead National Historical Park (National Park Service), about 3 miles west of Beatrice on Nebraska 4; the school has its own lot, mapped on OpenStreetMap. The park\'s hours page did not open in this session; hours not checked.',
  [W('Homestead National Historical Park'),NPSP('freeman-school','Freeman School')],th=[40.29285,-96.8419],thn='Freeman School lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='yes'),
N('gage-county-courthouse-beatrice','Gage County Courthouse','Beatrice','ne','civic','standing',40.271215,-96.746025,'exact','view','walk','','1890–1892',
  'The county courthouse at 612 Grant Street, built from 1890 to 1892 to designs by Gunn and Curtiss of Kansas City in the Richardsonian Romanesque style; M. T. Murphy was the contractor. It was renovated in 2008 and is on the National Register.',
  'A Richardsonian Romanesque county courthouse still in use. Materials and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working county courthouse; office hours were not read. Seen from the street; street parking in town, not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Gage County Courthouse')],brick='?'),
N('willa-cather-childhood-home-red-cloud','Willa Cather Childhood Home','Red Cloud','ne','landmark','standing',40.087166,-98.520731,'site','open','walk','','1878',
  'A frame house of 1878 at 241 North Cedar Street where the novelist Willa Cather grew up; her parents rented it and stayed until 1904. The foundation now named for her bought it in 1960, restored it to her years there and opened it to visitors in 1967. It is a National Historic Landmark and one of the sites of the Willa Cather State Historic Site.',
  'A story-and-a-half clapboard house with a porch across the front; parlor, dining room and bedrooms with period items; the stair from the kitchen to the attic is a reconstruction (Wikipedia). It reopened restored in December 2023. Shown on guided tours of about 30 minutes.',
  'The prairie house that Willa Cather wrote back into her novels.',
  'Owned by the Willa Cather Foundation. Tours start at the National Willa Cather Center on North Webster Street (its pages give both 413 and 425); the hours row is the Center\'s, from its own page read November 2026, and its tours page says tours run Tuesday to Saturday in summer. Street parking; not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Willa Cather House'),['Willa Cather Foundation: hours and directions','https://www.willacather.org/visit/hours-directions'],['Willa Cather Foundation: tours','https://willacather.org/tours']],brick='no',
  hours='Tours from the Cather Center. October to February: Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.; closed Sunday'),
N('buffalo-bill-ranch-north-platte','Buffalo Bill Ranch (Scout\'s Rest)','North Platte','ne','landmark','standing',41.16391,-100.79534,'exact','open','drive','','1878–1911',
  'William F. "Buffalo Bill" Cody\'s ranch, begun with 160 acres in 1878 and grown to 4,000 before it was sold in 1911. The Second Empire house was built in 1886; the barn of 1887 has "Scout\'s Rest Ranch" painted on its roof so it could be read from the Union Pacific a mile away. The state has kept 25 acres as a historical park since 1964; it is a National Historic Landmark (2021).',
  'Four original buildings: the 1886 mansion, the 1886 ice house, the cob house and the 1887 barn with its painted roof. The house and outbuildings can be toured, with a museum on Cody\'s life (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'Buffalo Bill Ranch State Historical Park, '+NGPC+', on Scouts Rest Ranch Road. '+PERMIT+'Visit North Platte\'s page (the town\'s visitor bureau, not the park) gives May 1 to October 31, Wednesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., the mansion closed in winter and the grounds open all year; the park\'s own page refused this session\'s tools. Parking not checked: the lot OpenStreetMap maps on Scouts Rest Ranch Road looked like rows of trees on the aerial.',
  [W('Buffalo Bill Ranch'),['Visit North Platte: Buffalo Bill Ranch','https://visitnorthplatte.com/directory-posts/buffalo-bill-state-historical-park/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('rock-creek-station-fairbury','Rock Creek Station','Fairbury','ne','landmark','traces',40.11389,-97.06417,'area','public','walk','','1857–1867',
  'A road ranch on the Oregon and California trails, set up in 1857. David McCanles bought it in 1859 and built a toll bridge over Rock Creek; it became a stage stop and Pony Express relay station, and in July 1861 the young stock tender James Butler Hickok shot McCanles there, the start of the "Wild Bill" legend. It served the Overland Stage until 1867.',
  'About 1,600 feet of deep wagon ruts on the hillside near the visitor center (National Park Service). The toll bridge and station buildings are reconstructions by the state.',
  'Where Wild Bill Hickok\'s legend began, with Oregon Trail ruts still cut in the hill.',
  'Rock Creek Station State Historical Park, '+NGPC+', 57426 710th Road, northeast of Endicott. '+PERMIT+'The National Park Service page gives the visitor center season as mid-April to mid-October, with a small museum fee; hours not checked on the park\'s own page. The pin is the Wikipedia coordinate; the entrance is at 40.11705, -97.06374 (National Park Service).',
  [W('Rock Creek Station'),NPSP('rock-creek-station-state-historical-park','Rock Creek Station')],th=[40.117051,-97.06374],thn='Park entrance (National Park Service coordinate)',brick='?'),
N('champion-mill','Champion Mill','Champion','ne','industry','standing',40.47064,-101.75055,'exact','open','drive','','1888–1968',
  'A flour and feed mill on Frenchman Creek, sited at a natural three-foot rock falls when the town (first called Hamilton) was laid out in 1886. Built in 1888, burned in December 1891 and rebuilt in 1892, it ground commercially until 1968. The mill, its 1887 earthen dam and headrace are a Register district.',
  'A three-story frame mill, the oldest parts from 1892, with its dam and headrace; Wikipedia calls it the state\'s last working water-powered mill. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Champion Mill State Historical Park; Wikipedia says Chase County runs the mill as a museum and park. Hours were not found on a page of its own (the county page answered 404). The pin is the mill building mapped on OpenStreetMap; parking not checked, no lot is mapped.',
  [W('Champion Mill (Nebraska)')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('joslyn-castle-omaha','Joslyn Castle','Omaha','ne','landmark','standing',41.2616,-95.971887,'exact','open','walk','','1903',
  'George and Sarah Joslyn\'s 35-room Scottish Baronial mansion, "Lynhurst", built in eleven months in 1903 of Kansas limestone to designs by John McDonald, with grounds by Jens Jensen. George Joslyn made his money in ready-print newspapers; Sarah\'s gift built the Joslyn Art Museum. The 1913 Easter tornado wrecked part of the grounds.',
  'The four-story stone house and its 1901 carriage-house tower on five acres of gardens; a bridge from before the 1913 tornado survives and the lake\'s outline can still be seen (Wikipedia). Tours take in the main, second and third floors.',
  '',
  'Joslyn Castle & Gardens, 3902 Davenport Street. Inside only on docent-led tours (about 75 to 90 minutes, tickets on its page); the gardens and grounds are open daily from sunrise to sunset (its own tours page, read November 2026). A café in the carriage house opens weekdays.',
  [W('Joslyn Castle'),['Joslyn Castle: public tours','https://www.joslyncastle.com/tours']],brick='no',
  hours='Tours at noon Sunday, Monday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday; no tours Tuesday or Saturday. Gardens daily sunrise to sunset'),
N('norris-house-mccook','Senator George Norris House','McCook','ne','landmark','standing',40.20412,-100.626074,'exact','open','walk','','1899',
  'The house George W. Norris bought in 1899, early in a career of five House terms and thirty years in the Senate, in which he backed the New Deal, the Tennessee Valley Authority and rural electrification. His widow gave it to the state historical society in 1968; it is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A story-and-a-half stuccoed frame house with Stick-style trim in the gables and a round-arched entrance, furnished mainly with the Norris family\'s own things (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'Senator George Norris State Historic Site, History Nebraska, 706 Norris Avenue; admission free; no public restrooms, no food or drink (its own page, read November 2026). Street parking; Norris Park is opposite.',
  [W('George W. Norris House'),HN('senator-george-norris-state-historic-site','Senator George Norris State Historic Site')],brick='no',
  hours='Thursday to Saturday 1 to 5 p.m., or by appointment'),
N('fort-kearny-state-historical-park','Fort Kearny','Kearney','ne','fort','traces',40.643631,-99.004988,'site','public','walk','forts','1848–1871',
  'The first of the Army\'s forts on the Oregon-California trail, begun in 1848 on the south bank of the Platte: an unwalled cluster of adobe and sod buildings where emigrants resupplied, swapped stock and posted letters. It was a Pony Express and stage station in 1860 and 1861 and closed in 1871, when its buildings were torn down or left to melt away. The state took 40 acres of the site in 1929.',
  'The earth embankment of the 1864 fortification is still visible (Wikipedia), with cottonwoods round the old parade ground; reconstructed sod and adobe buildings, a blacksmith shop and an interpretive center (National Park Service).',
  '',
  'Fort Kearny State Historical Park, '+NGPC+', 1020 V Road southeast of Kearney. '+PERMIT+'Wikipedia says the park opens only in summer; hours not checked on the park\'s own page. Parking: the lot by the entrance.',
  [W('Fort Kearny'),NPSP('000/fort-kearny-state-historical-park','Fort Kearny State Historical Park')],th=[40.64348,-99.00439],thn='Park lot',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('kennard-house-lincoln','Thomas P. Kennard House','Lincoln','ne','landmark','standing',40.806498,-96.697626,'exact','view','walk','','1869',
  'The oldest building left in Lincoln\'s original plat, an Italianate house of 1869 by the Chicago architect John Keys Winchell for Thomas P. Kennard, Nebraska\'s first secretary of state and one of the three men who chose the site of the capital. It became the Nebraska Statehood Memorial in 1965.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story stuccoed brick house with a frame cupola on its low hip roof, restored to its first look after heavy changes (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'Thomas P. Kennard State Historic Site, History Nebraska, 1627 H Street. Its own page (read November 2026) says the house is not open for tours at present: see it from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Thomas P. Kennard House'),HN('thomas-p-kennard-state-historic-site/','Thomas P. Kennard State Historic Site')],brick='no'),
N('fort-sidney','Fort Sidney','Sidney','ne','fort','standing',41.144233,-102.971762,'site','open','walk','forts','1867–1894',
  'Set up as Sidney Station in 1867 to guard Union Pacific track layers, it was rebuilt below the bluffs on Lodgepole Creek with buildings moved from Fort Sedgwick, Colorado, named Fort Sidney about 1879, and served as the railhead for the Sidney-Black Hills Trail. It closed in 1894.',
  'Three buildings of a post that once had 40 (Western Nebraska Tourism Coalition): the married officers\' quarters, which hold the Cheyenne County Museum, the commander\'s home with late 19th-century furnishings, and a powder house (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Museums of the fort complex at 544 Jackson Street. The Western Nebraska Tourism Coalition gives the season as Memorial Day to Labor Day; days and times not checked on a page of the museum\'s own. Street parking.',
  [W('Fort Sidney'),['Western Nebraska Tourism Coalition: Fort Sidney Museum','https://westnebraska.com/places/fort-sidney-museum/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('omaha-union-station-durham-museum','Omaha Union Station (Durham Museum)','Omaha','ne','rail','standing',41.251488,-95.928995,'exact','open','walk','stations','1929–1931',
  'The Union Pacific\'s first Art Deco station, by Gilbert Stanley Underwood, built from 1929 to January 1931 on a steel frame clad in cream glazed terra cotta, in place of an 1899 brick depot. It became the home of the Durham Museum, which added a wing in a 1997 renovation; it is a National Historic Landmark (2016).',
  'The Art Deco station hall and building, now the museum (Wikipedia). Original interior fittings not checked in detail.',
  'An Art Deco railroad palace on the Tenth Street viaduct.',
  'The Durham Museum, 801 South 10th Street. Admission charged; hours change month by month and holidays close it (its own page, read November 2026). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Omaha Union Station'),['Durham Museum: hours and admission','https://durhammuseum.org/visit/hours-and-admission/']],brick='no',
  hours='November: Tuesday, Wednesday, Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Friday 10 a.m. to 8 p.m., Sunday noon to 4 p.m.; closed Monday, Thursday'),
]

